"At the beginning of the surah, Allah swears by the sky and by al-Tariq. The oath is meant to draw attention to the importance of the truth about to be explained. Just as the sky refers to the known heaven, it can also encompass everything in the heights. Al-Tariq means that which comes by night, knocks at the door, or strikes the heart; here, the bright star that rises toward morning is meant.\n\nIn the second and third verses, what al-Tariq is is asked and answered: It is the star piercing the darkness (necm-i sâkıb). Sâkıb means to pierce or to ascend; this star cleaves the darkness with its light. Some commentators have said that this is the Morning Star, while others have said it is the Pleiades (Süreyya) or another star. Moreover, in a spiritual sense, it can also represent divine guidance that imparts light to the heart.\n\nIn the fourth verse, it is stated that over every soul there is a protector (hâfız). This protector is primarily Allah; furthermore, the angels accompanying man also record his deeds and protect him. No human being is left unattended; everyone is under surveillance at every moment. This demonstrates Allah's power and mercy.\n\nIn the fifth verse, man is commanded to look at what he was created from. Man was created from an insignificant fluid, that is, an ejected semen. While this reminds him of how humble his origin is, Allah's creating him from this simple matter and elevating him is also evidence of His power to create anew.\n\nIn the sixth and seventh verses, the nature of that fluid is described: An ejected fluid (mâin dâfik) that emerges from between the backbone (sulb) of men and the ribs (terâib) of women. Sulb is the spine, while terâib is the ribcage. This expression points to the region where the reproductive organs are located.\n\nCommentators present two views regarding this verse: According to the first view, only the semen of the man is meant. According to the second view, both the male and female fluids should be considered together, because the child is formed from the union of both. The woman's fluid is thin and yellow, lacking the characteristic of being ejected with force, yet it plays a role in fertilization.\n\nAfter this initial stage of creation, man sequentially passes through the stages of nutfe (drop), alaka (embryo), mudga (morsel of flesh), bones, and being clothed with flesh, reaching an entirely different creation in the seventh stage. By showing these stages, Allah emphasizes that He has the power to resurrect man once again.\n\nIn summary, these verses call upon man to reflect on the wisdom in human creation. Even though man was created from a simple fluid, there is a constant protector over him. This should distance him from arrogance and direct him toward Allah. Furthermore, he should believe in Allah's power to resurrect after death.\n\nElmalılı addresses the objections raised against the expression \"emerges from between the backbone and the breastbones\" in Surah al-Tariq. These objections are based on the misunderstanding that semen is formed directly in the testicles. However, the expression carries a broad meaning encompassing the entire reproductive process, such as the formation, differentiation, and ejection of semen for fertilization. Elmalılı states that these objections are empty sophistries, because the expression in question is the most comprehensive description embracing all the relevant organs and the nervous system in both the male and female bodies.\n\nThe expression \"ejected fluid\" in the verse implies not only the ejection of the fluid, but also that it must be discharged toward the womb for fertilization. The maturation of semen and its discharge for fertilization takes place between the backbone and the breastbones in both man and woman. Therefore, this stage of creation is an important proof demonstrating Allah's power and man's helplessness.\n\nThe creation of man from a nutfe reveals the power of the Supreme Creator, Who made him a thinking, reasoning being. It also proves that resurrection in the Hereafter is possible. For Allah, Who created a human being from scattered parts, is also able to bring them together again. This is evidence of the resurrection after death and the Day of Reckoning.\n\nThe day when secrets will be examined is the day when the beliefs, intentions, and deeds hidden in hearts will be exposed. On that day, a person will have neither power nor helper. If the revealed secrets are pure, there is salvation; if they are defiled, there is punishment. Therefore, man should reflect upon what he was created from and keep his secrets pure during worldly life.\n\nThe surah begins by swearing by the returning sky and the earth that splits open. The expression \"the returning sky\" points to the rotational movements of the sky, rain, or the descending and ascending of the angels. As for \"the earth that splits open,\" it refers to meanings such as the emergence of plants, valleys, or the cleaving open of graves. These oaths recall the cycle in human creation and the resurrection in the Hereafter.\n\nThe Quran is not a joke or amusement, but a decisive word that distinguishes truth from falsehood. Even if the disbelievers devise plots to invalidate the Quran, Allah's plan is stronger than theirs. What falls upon the believers is to heed this solemn word and strive to enter Allah's presence with a sound heart on the day when secrets are disclosed. As for the disbelievers, they have been granted a brief respite."